<header class="page-head"><div class="eyebrow">Safe, practical next steps</div><h1>What to Do After a Hailstorm</h1><p>Use this sequence to check your vehicle carefully, understand weather reports, and make an informed repair decision.</p></header>
<article class="section narrow prose">
<h2>1. Wait until the storm is safely over</h2><p>Remain sheltered while severe weather is active. Follow National Weather Service warnings and local emergency instructions, and do not go outside to protect or inspect a vehicle during hail, lightning, high wind, or flooding. Peak Country’s weather pages are informational and do not replace official warnings.</p>
<h2>2. Look at the vehicle in good lighting</h2><p>When conditions are safe, walk around the vehicle without climbing onto it. Look across the hood, roof, trunk or liftgate, roof rails, doors, fenders, and quarter panels. Directional light and clean reflections make dents easier to see because a low spot bends a straight reflected line. Changing your viewing angle often reveals marks that disappear when viewed directly.</p>
<h2>3. Do not assume no visible damage means no damage</h2><p>Shallow hail dents can be difficult to identify outdoors, especially on light-colored paint, dusty panels, curved surfaces, or under broad overcast light. Trim can also conceal the edge of a damaged area. A quick driveway check is useful, but it is not the same as inspecting clean panels under controlled PDR lighting.</p>
<h2>4. Check nearby official hail reports</h2><p>The <a href="/hail-tracker/">Peak Country Hail Tracker</a> shows current official-source context, while the <a href="/northern-colorado-hail-history/">Northern Colorado Hail History</a> provides verified NOAA/NCEI observations from completed seasons. The <a href="/hail-size-guide/">Hail Size Guide</a> explains common diameter comparisons. A nearby hail report does not prove that a particular vehicle was damaged. Reports have varying location precision, and hail can vary sharply over short distances.</p>
<h2>5. Photograph obvious damage</h2><p>Take wide photographs of each affected side and closer images of obvious dents, broken trim, or glass damage. Record the date, approximate time, vehicle location, and any safe observations about the storm. Avoid placing objects on the paint to mark dents. Photographs can preserve useful context, but they may not reveal the full dent count or repairability.</p>
<h2>6. Request a professional inspection</h2><p>A professional hail inspection uses controlled reflection lighting and a panel-by-panel process. The technician evaluates dent depth, location, metal stretch, paint condition, panel material, previous repairs, and access behind the panel. This establishes actual visible damage and helps determine whether <a href="/auto-hail-repair/">Paintless Dent Repair or another repair method</a> is appropriate.</p>
<h2>7. Consider insurance without rushing</h2><p>You may choose to contact your insurer and should follow the requirements of your policy. Peak Country works with insurance hail claims, and an inspection can help document visible damage. Sometimes additional legitimate damage becomes visible after proper access or limited disassembly; when that happens, the repair file may require further documentation and supplement handling. Coverage and claim decisions remain with the insurer.</p>
<h2>8. Understand the repair plan</h2><p>Before authorizing work, ask which panels are suitable for PDR, whether the paint is intact, what access or trim removal is expected, and what realistic result the technician anticipates. Review Peak Country’s <a href="/repair-standards/">repair standards</a> and <a href="/faq/">hail-repair FAQ</a>. Avoid pressing, heating, or glue-pulling dents yourself, since an unsuccessful attempt can complicate later repair.</p>
<h2>Keep the vehicle safe while deciding</h2><p>If glass is broken or a panel has sharp damage, avoid handling loose fragments and arrange appropriate protection or transport. Do not cover a wet vehicle with material that can rub grit across the paint, and do not test dents with household suction tools, boiling water, dry ice, or improvised glue. These approaches can harm paint, trim, wiring, or panel shape and can make the original damage harder to evaluate.</p><p>Keep copies of photographs, estimates, and repair discussions in one place. If an insurer is involved, record claim contacts and ask questions when a proposed operation is unclear. Documentation supports an orderly process; it does not replace inspection, repair planning, or a final quality review.</p>
